.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH CLRI 1M
.SH NAME
clri \- clear i-node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/clri
.I filesystem i-number ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Clri
writes zeros on the i-nodes
with the decimal
.I i-numbers
on the
.I filesystem.
After
.I clri,
any blocks
in the affected file
will show up as `missing' in an
.IR icheck (1M)
of the
.I filesystem.
.PP
Read and write permission is required on the specified
file system device.
The i-node becomes allocatable.
.PP
The primary purpose of this routine
is to remove a file which
for some reason appears in no
directory.
If it is used to zap an i-node
which does appear in a directory, care should be taken to track down
the entry and remove it.
Otherwise, when the i-node is reallocated to some new file,
the old entry will still point to that file.
At that point removing the old entry will destroy the new file.
The new entry will again point to an unallocated i-node,
so the whole cycle is likely to be repeated again and again.
.PP
.B Note:
.IR fsck (1M)
is the modern way to do all of that.
It finds a file that appears in no directory for itself and offers to reconnect it into
.BR /lost+found ,
and it removes an entry naming an unallocated i-node rather than leaving the cycle above to
be broken by hand.
.I Clri
is worth reaching for when the file is to be thrown away rather than kept.
.PP
Every
.I i-number
is checked, and the block it lies in read, before
.B anything
is written: a run that is going to fail on its fourth argument fails before it has cleared
the first three.
.PP
.B Note:
an
.I i-number
outside the file system's i-list is
.BR refused .
v7 seeks wherever the number names, so
.B "clri /dev/rmd0 99999"
there writes 3072 bytes of zeros over a
.B data
block \- somebody's file \- with no diagnostic at all.
This version reads the super-block, works out how many i-numbers the volume has, and refuses
anything outside 2 through that.
I-node 1 goes with them: it exists and
.IR ialloc ()
can never hand it out.
.PP
.B Note:
if the
.I filesystem
is the mounted root,
.I clri
prints
.B "***** BOOT UNIX (NO SYNC!) *****"
and stops, as
.IR fsck (1M)
does.
That is what the BUGS section below means in this system's terms.
.SH FILES
/dev/rmd0, /dev/rmd1
.SH "SEE ALSO"
icheck(1M), dcheck(1M), ncheck(1M), fsck(1M),
filsys(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`\fIarg\fR: is not a number' and `\fIarg\fR: is zero' are v7's.
`\fIarg\fR: out of range 2..\fIn\fR' is the check above.
.PP
`\fIfilesystem\fR: not a filesystem' means the super-block carries no
.I FS_MAGIC
and `filesystem geometry mismatch' that it describes a block size, an i-nodes-per-block or
an addresses-per-inode this system does not have.
Either way there is no i-list to bound an
.I i-number
against, so nothing is cleared.
.SH BUGS
If the file is open,
.I clri
is likely to be ineffective.
.PP
.B Note:
specifically, the kernel holds the i-node in core while the file is open and
.IR iput ()
writes that copy back to the disk when the last reference goes away \- over the zeros this
program wrote.
The same thing on a larger scale is why the mounted root stops the machine rather than
returning: the whole in-core i-node cache is newer than the disk.
